name			rtl8814au
version			1.0.0-1
architecture	x86_64
summary			"RTL8814AU USB WiFi driver (unofficial)"

description		"Native Haiku kernel driver for USB WiFi adapters based on
the Realtek RTL8814AU chipset (4x4 802.11ac, used in adapters like the
ASUS USB-AC68 and Edimax EW-7833UAC).

Open and WPA2-PSK (AES/CCMP) networks work on both 2.4 GHz and 5 GHz, by
any of Haiku's normal connection routes: the Deskbar network menu, the
Network preferences panel, or

  ifconfig /dev/net/rtl8814au/0 join <ssid> <passphrase>
  ifconfig /dev/net/rtl8814au/0 auto-config

A bundled wifi-join helper is also included. It hands the passphrase
straight to the driver and runs the four-way handshake in the kernel,
which needs no net_server round trip and is useful for scripting and for
diagnosing the driver in isolation. Both routes are supported and end in
the same place.

CCMP encrypt and decrypt for the data path are performed in software
inside the driver; the chip's hardware crypto engine has not been made to
engage on Haiku and the software fallback is what makes WPA2 work end to
end.

Known limitations, measured rather than estimated: a join can fail and
need retrying, at about 5 percent from a fresh boot; repeatedly taking
the interface down and up makes this much worse and a reboot clears it;
throughput is 16-19 Mbit/s receive and 9-11 Mbit/s transmit on 5 GHz,
which is a consequence of running 20 MHz channels with no frame
aggregation and no transmit rate adaptation rather than a defect. Open
unencrypted networks and auto-connect at boot are limited by Haiku
itself, not by this driver. See the changelog for the full list.

Only the ASUS USB-AC68 and Edimax EW-7833UAC have been tested on real
hardware. Seven further USB IDs are claimed because they appear in the
reference driver's RTL8814AU table, and are untested.

This driver is NOT part of the official Haiku distribution. It is
developed independently and shipped as a standalone package. Bug reports
welcome at https://github.com/KevinAdams05/RTL8814AU_Haiku/issues."

